<mujoco>
  <extension>
    <plugin plugin="mujoco.elasticity.cable"/>
  </extension>
  <worldbody>
    <!-- EVA/EPE pool noodle cut to 1.2 m: 65 mm OD, 30 mm bore, 94 g, foam
         E ~ 0.8 MPa, nu ~ 0.3. MuJoCo's cable composite is a chain of solid
         capsules, so every number here is the solid-capsule equivalent of that
         hollow section, calibrated rather than guessed:
           density  gives a 94 g chain. It depends on count, because
                    consecutive capsule end caps overlap and double-count mass.
           bend     makes a 1.2 m cantilever of this chain droop 0.305 m, which
                    is what E = 0.8 MPa predicts for the real section. (The old
                    bend="1e5" drooped 1.018 m - that is a rope, not a noodle.)
           twist    = bend / (2 * (1 + nu)).

         count must satisfy  size/(count-1) >= 2*size_of_geom.  Capsules i and
         i+2 are born interpenetrating whenever the chain pitch falls below the
         capsule diameter, and the composite only excludes ADJACENT pairs. The
         old count="25" gave a 50 mm pitch against a 60 mm diameter, so every
         i/i+2 pair sat 10 mm inside its neighbour for the whole run: 44
         permanent contacts fighting the elasticity plugin, which is what made
         the rod itself visibly misbehave. At count="13" the pitch is 100 mm.
         The coarser chain also drops the stiffest bending mode from 240 Hz to
         85 Hz (omega*dt 1.51 -> 0.54, explicit limit ~2) at the scene world's
         1 ms <option timestep>.

         The chain itself (mass, bend, twist) behaves identically on MuJoCo
         2.3.7 and 3.3.5: a joint-clamped cantilever of it settles to the same
         tip droop on both. Only the end welds below had to be retuned. -->
    <composite prefix="noodle/" type="cable" curve="s" count="13 1 1"
               size="1.2" offset="-0.6 0 0.2" initial="free">
      <plugin plugin="mujoco.elasticity.cable">
        <config key="twist" value="2.0e5"/>
        <config key="bend" value="5.2e5"/>
      </plugin>
      <!-- Foam is strongly dissipative (tan_delta ~ 0.1). These dampers act in
           series, so the whole-noodle value is damping/(count-1); 0.05 here is
           ~6% on the rod's torsional mode. Rescale it with count. -->
      <joint kind="main" damping="0.05"/>
      <!-- Bit 30 marks the connector. conaffinity="1" collides with the floor
           but not with itself; self-collision may be re-enabled with
           conaffinity="1073741825" as long as the count rule above holds. -->
      <geom type="capsule" size="0.0325" density="16.47" rgba="0.9 0.5 0.1 1"
            contype="1073741824" conaffinity="1"/>
    </composite>
  </worldbody>
  <equality>
    <!-- Clamped ends, matching the real mount. A weld transmits moment, which
         couples both drones' attitude loops.

         MuJoCo 3 retuning. MuJoCo 3.0 fixed weld efc_diagApprox (and 3.0.1 the
         torquescale handling). A weld's constraint stiffness now scales with
         the effective inertia of its LIGHTER body, here a 7.8 g end capsule,
         and torquescale no longer sets the moment it transmits. The 2.3.7
         values torquescale="0.1" solref="0.08 1" therefore turned into a pin
         on 3.3.5. Measured with one end held and a 0.965 kg free body on the
         other (axial k in N/m, rotational k in Nm/rad, x along the rod):

                                       k_axial  k_lateral  k_rx   k_ry
           2.3.7 ts=0.1 tc=0.08          22      0.66     0.20   0.29
           3.3.5 ts=0.1 tc=0.08          22      0.42     6e-5   0.004
           3.3.5 ts=1   tc=0.007       1500      0.64     0.18   0.27

         The last row is used here. Its rotational and lateral stiffness match
         the clamp that was tuned and flown on 2.3.7, and k_ry is close to the
         limit set by the rod's own bending. Its axial stiffness, 1.5 kN/m, is
         close to the ~1.7 kN/m estimated for the real foam. On 2.3.7 the welds
         made the rod axially soft (22 N/m), which was an artifact. Axial
         stiffness goes as 1/timeconst^2 (tc=0.01 gives 0.94 kN/m). Keep
         timeconst at or above 2 steps (0.002). The 2.3.7 torquescale x
         timeconst stability map does not transfer to MuJoCo 3.

         anchor is relative to body2, so "0 0 0" puts the weld point on each
         drone's base_link origin, which is where the rod ends. relpose is
         omitted, so MuJoCo fills it from qpos0 and the weld starts satisfied.
         Swap weld -> connect for pinned ends (force only, no clamp moment). -->
    <weld name="noodle_left" body1="noodle/B_first" body2="elastic1/base_link"
          anchor="0 0 0" torquescale="1" solref="0.007 1"/>
    <weld name="noodle_right" body1="noodle/B_last" body2="elastic2/base_link"
          anchor="0 0 0" torquescale="1" solref="0.007 1"/>
  </equality>
</mujoco>
